INT. AANYA'S BEDROOM - DAY

The bedroom is quiet. It is a sanctuary of order. 

Shelves hold jars of sorted stones. One jar holds only white quartz. Another holds gray slate. 

Aanya (8) sits at her desk. She is an autistic girl. She has straight black hair in a low braid. She wears a mustard yellow sweater. The sleeves are pulled warm over her hands. Her gray leggings are neat and comfortable.

She reaches into her pocket. She pulls out her totem, a smooth gray pebble. She turns it in her palm. It is cool and grounding.

Aanya picks up a small digital audio recorder. She checks the battery level. Three bars. Perfect.

She wants to record the park fountain. She wants sixty seconds of clean, uninterrupted water rhythm. It is her goal for the day.

She slips the recorder into her pocket next to the pebble.

EXT. OAK STREET PARK - DAY

Sunny and bright. The park is a tapestry of green grass and concrete paths. 

In the center sits the historic stone fountain. Water cascades over three tiers. 

Splash. Ripple. Splash. Ripple.

To Aanya, the sound is a beautiful, repeating wave. It looks like blue hills in her mind.

Aanya sits on a wooden bench near the fountain. She puts her noise-reducing headphones around her neck. She does not need them yet. The park is quiet.

She pulls out her audio recorder. She points the microphone at the splashing water. 

She presses RECORD.

The digital timer ticks up.

One second. Two seconds. Three seconds.

Splash. Ripple. Splash. Ripple.

Twenty seconds. Twenty-one seconds.

A sharp, metallic sound cuts through the air.

CLICK. CHIRP.

Aanya flinches. Her shoulders tighten. 

Across the street, the pedestrian crossing signal flashes. It is a bright orange hand. 

FLASH. FLASH. FLASH. Pause.

FLASH. FLASH.

Then silence. The hand stays dark.

Aanya stops her recording. She sighs. The perfect loop is ruined. 

But she does not walk away. She stares at the crossing signal. 

Her mind registers the sequence. 

Three flashes. A two-second pause. Two flashes. 

It should be a steady, alternating beat. Five flashes, five seconds apart. 

She pulls a small green notebook from her pocket. She writes: "Oak Street. 3-2-2. The rhythm is broken."

EXT. MAPLE STREET CROSSING - NEXT DAY

Aanya walks to school. The sidewalk is clean. Yellow autumn leaves lie in neat lines near the curb.

She reaches the corner of Maple Street. 

She stops. She watches the crossing signal.

CHIRP. FLASH. FLASH. FLASH. FLASH. Pause.

FLASH. FLASH.

Four flashes. A two-second pause. Two flashes. 

She pulls out her notebook. She compares today's entry with yesterday's.

Oak Street: 3-2-2. 
Maple Street: 4-2-2.

The first number is growing. The pause is constant. The final number is constant.

Aanya feels a spark of excitement. It is a pattern. It is a math puzzle waiting to be solved.

INT. CLASSROOM - DAY

Mr. Harrison writes multiplication tables on the whiteboard. The squeak of the marker is loud.

Many kids are whispering. The room feels warm and crowded. 

Aanya sits at her desk. She does not look at the whiteboard. She looks at her green notebook. 

She has drawn a grid of the town's streets.

Oak Street is coordinate A-1. Maple Street is coordinate B-2. 

The line of decay is moving south-east.

Mr. Harrison walks over to her desk. He looks down at her notebook.

MR. HARRISON
Aanya, we are on page twelve. Please open your math book.

AANYA
(without looking up)
The town crossing signals are losing their beat, Mr. Harrison. It is a descending sequence. It is moving toward School Street.

MR. HARRISON
They are just old bulbs, Aanya. The city maintenance crew will replace them. Now, let us focus on multiplication.

Aanya touches the smooth gray pebble in her pocket. 

AANYA
But the crew does not know the sequence. If they do not fix the root controller, the system will fail completely. School Street is next.

MR. HARRISON
(softly)
That is enough about the lights, Aanya. Please do your work.

Aanya opens her math book. She does not feel bad. She knows her math is correct. The adults just do not see the pattern yet.

EXT. TOWN SQUARE - AFTERNOON

Aanya walks toward the Town Maintenance Office. 

She passes the bakery. She passes the library.

She notices the shadows of the trees. They make perfect parallel lines on the gray concrete.

She steps carefully, ensuring her sneakers only touch the gray pavement, avoiding the cracks. 

INT. TOWN MAINTENANCE OFFICE - CONTINUOUS

The office is small and smells of old paper and floor wax.

Mr. Miller, a man with a mustache and a blue shirt, sits behind a high wooden counter.

He is typing on a loud keyboard.

CLACK. CLACK. DING.

Aanya walks to the counter. She stands quietly until Mr. Miller looks down.

MR. MILLER
Can I help you, young lady?

AANYA
The School Street crossing signal will fail tomorrow morning at exactly 8:15 AM.

Mr. Miller blinks. He stops typing.

MR. MILLER
Excuse me?

AANYA
(pointing to her notebook)
I have mapped the decay. Oak Street failed on Monday. Maple Street glitched on Tuesday. The interval of decay is decreasing by twenty percent every twenty-four hours. School Street is the apex of the grid. It will go dark during school drop-off.

Mr. Miller looks at the notebook. He sees the neat grids and formulas. He shakes his head with a small, patronizing smile.

MR. MILLER
That is some fancy drawing, kid. But our signals are on a digital timer. They do not have a rhythm. You are overthinking this. Why don't you run along and play like a normal kid?

Aanya's fingers tighten around the pebble in her pocket. 

AANYA
The digital timer is the problem. The central clock chip is dropping frames. It is like a skipping song.

MR. MILLER
Go home, kid. We have got real work to do here.

Aanya looks at him. Her eyes are steady and calm.

AANYA
I am telling the truth. The pattern does not lie.

She turns and walks out of the office. She does not run. She keeps her pace steady.

INT. AANYA'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The room is dark except for a yellow desk lamp. 

Aanya sits on her bed. Her noise-reducing headphones are on. They keep the night sounds of wind and cars outside.

She looks at her colorful wall chart. 

She knows she is right. She does not feel sad about what Mr. Miller said. She just feels a strong sense of duty. 

If the light fails at 8:15 AM, the kids crossing School Street will be in danger. The cars drive very fast down the hill.

She must make the pattern visible to everyone.

She opens her backpack.

She packs her digital audio recorder.

She packs a portable speaker.

She packs a large roll of high-visibility blue tape.

She turns her smooth gray pebble one last time, then places it under her pillow.

EXT. SCHOOL STREET CROSSING - MORNING

7:55 AM.

The intersection of School Street and Pine Avenue is a storm of activity.

Yellow school buses rumble past. Cars honk. Parents shout goodbyes.

To Aanya, the noise is a jagged red wall. It hurts her ears.

She pulls her noise-reducing headphones over her ears. The red wall crumbles into a soft, manageable gray hum.

She walks to the curb.

Mrs. Gable, the school crossing guard, stands on the corner. She wears a bright orange vest and holds a stop sign.

MRS. GABLE
Morning, Aanya! Big day today?

Aanya does not answer. She is looking at the overhead pedestrian signal.

It is flashing. But the flashes are weak.

FLASH. Pause. FLASH. 

The interval is down to one second. The system is choking.

Aanya drops her backpack. She pulls out the roll of blue tape.

She kneels on the concrete. She sticks a long, straight line of blue tape across the sidewalk, three feet behind the curb.

Mrs. Gable watches her, confused.

MRS. GABLE
What are you doing with that tape, sweetie?

AANYA
(loudly, over her headphones)
This is the safety line. Nobody must cross this line until the signal is clear. The light is about to die.

MRS. GABLE
The light is fine, Aanya. It is blinking right now.

Aanya looks at her watch. 

8:12 AM.

She places her portable speaker on a brick ledge near the crossing. She plugs her audio recorder into the speaker.

She cues up a file on her recorder: "Perfect crossing signal loop.wav."

She waits.

EXT. SCHOOL STREET CROSSING - 8:14 AM

More kids arrive. They crowd the corner.

Leo (7) and Chloe (8) stand near the curb. They are laughing and playing with a red ball.

Aanya steps in front of them. She points to the blue tape on the concrete.

AANYA
Stand behind the blue line, please. It is not safe yet.

LEO
Why? The light says we can go.

He points up. The walking man icon is lit. But it is flickering rapidly.

AANYA
The light is lying. Look at the pattern.

Suddenly, the signal goes black.

Not just the walking man. The traffic light for the cars goes black too. All four corners of the intersection go dark at once.

The central clock chip has failed.

EXT. SCHOOL STREET CROSSING - 8:15 AM

At the top of the School Street hill, a large blue delivery truck rumbles downward.

The driver does not see any lights. He assumes he has the green light. He does not slow down.

Mrs. Gable is looking down at her clipboard. She does not see the truck.

Leo drops his red ball. It bounces over the curb and into the street.

LEO
My ball!

He steps over the blue tape.

EXT. SCHOOL STREET CROSSING - CONTINUOUS

Aanya does not hesitate.

She hits PLAY on her audio recorder.

The portable speaker blasts a loud, clear, rhythmic sound.

CHIRP. CHIRP. CHIRP.

It is the perfect, steady crossing audio loop Aanya recorded at the park.

It is the exact sound the kids hear every day when it is safe to stop and look.

Leo freezes. He hears the familiar sound and looks at Aanya.

Aanya stands on the blue tape. She holds her hands up high, palms flat toward the street.

AANYA
STOP!

Mrs. Gable hears the loud audio and spins around. She sees the delivery truck speeding down the hill.

She blows her whistle. 

TRRRRRT!

She jumps into the street, waving her stop sign.

The truck driver hears the whistle and the loud speaker. He sees Aanya's bright blue tape and Mrs. Gable's sign.

He slams on his brakes.

SCREEEEECH!

The heavy truck slides on the asphalt. It comes to a stop three feet from the crosswalk.

Silence falls over the intersection.

Only the steady, artificial sound from Aanya's speaker remains.

CHIRP. CHIRP. CHIRP.

EXT. SCHOOL STREET CROSSING - MOMENTS LATER

Parents and kids gasp. 

Mrs. Gable walks over to the truck driver, her chest rising and falling.

MRS. GABLE
Are you okay?

TRUCK DRIVER
I didn't see any lights! They just went black. Thank God for that speaker sound. I would have never stopped.

Mrs. Gable turns to Aanya. 

She looks at the blue tape. She looks at the speaker.

MRS. GABLE
Aanya. You knew. You really knew.

Aanya turns off her recorder. She pulls her headphones down around her neck.

AANYA
The math is always correct, Mrs. Gable.

Mr. Miller walks up the sidewalk. He is holding a tool bag. He looks at the dead signal, then at Aanya.

He looks down at his boots, his face turning red.

MR. MILLER
I... I checked the logs after you left. You were right about the clock chip, kid. I should have listened to you. I am sorry.

AANYA
It is okay, Mr. Miller. But please use the gold-plated chips next time. They hold the rhythm better.

Mr. Miller smiles and nods.

MR. MILLER
Gold-plated. Got it. I will write that down.

EXT. OAK STREET PARK - NEXT DAY

The sun is warm. 

The Oak Street crossing signal flashes in a perfect, steady five-beat rhythm.

FLASH. FLASH. FLASH. FLASH. FLASH.

Aanya sits on her favorite green bench near the fountain.

She is a proud autistic girl, exactly as she was yesterday. She does not need to change. The world just needed to listen to her rhythm.

She reaches into her pocket and turns her smooth gray pebble. It is warm from the sun.

She pulls out her audio recorder. 

The fountain splashes.

Splash. Ripple. Splash. Ripple.

She presses RECORD.

One second. Two seconds. Three seconds.

No static. No glitches. Just the beautiful, endless loop of water.

Aanya smiles. She closes her eyes and listens.

FADE OUT.